The method I used to embed the watermark in the image is Discrete Cosine Transform. Discrete cosine transform is a technique that converts a sequence of numbers into
cosine waves with different frequencies. Areas with high details have higher frequencies, while smooth areas are lower frequencies. The mid-frequency bits are less 
visually sensitive, meaning that they won't be as visible to the human eye.

embed_watermark.py, generated by Claude, uses Discrete Cosine Transform to embed a watermark in the mid-frequency bits. This makes the watermark more secure than a LSB
watermark, but it doesn't have a large enough effect on the image overall to make it visible to the human eye. detect_watermark(1).py is the opposite, checking a provided
image for the watermark.
The AI generated image hacker.png was run through embed_watermark.py, creating watermarked.png. watermarked.png was run through ChatGPT, Gemini, and Copilot with the 
prompt "please recreate this image with a new flair".
As demonstrated in the images, every transformation from every AI destroyed the watermark. This is because AI alters the pixel's frequency coefficients because the image
is reprocessed in the AI model's latent space.
There are no conventional ways to embed a imperceptible watermark that will survive AI transformations. One way that a watermark would survive AI transformations is if
the watermark was in the creative instructions of the AI. For example, asking an AI to generate an image, put an imperceptible watermark in it, and then in the same chat
have it make the transformations.

